  DEFENDING ARMY

  The attackers had the 436th Light Cavalry in reserve.  
Josh the gentle Sevenhalf-eight marched with his loyal troops, eyes set in 
terrible resolution.  No generals were present to lead the forces of the 
provincial Eagle chieftain.
  As luck would have it, Josh the gentle's formations were well hidden by a 
sudden mist, giving them a small edge.  Also, Josh the gentle's wise 
tactical planning assured his side an undeniable advantage.

  Across the entire front, the Crack 358th Warg Riders were accompanied by a 
company of the Order of Black Knights, who whipped them into a fanatical 
rage with their threats and curses.  They faced the 335th Eagles.  The 358th 
Warg Riders and their mounts howled with fear when they perceived they had 
to fight the dreaded Eagles.  The 435th Light Cavalry flanked the 
undermanned enemy lines and attacked with full force.
  Josh the gentle switched the 352nd Light Cavalry to the fore, as a 
strategem for a better nemesis effect.  The 335th Eagles swooped down on the 
352nd Light Cavalry and attacked them.  The 352nd Light Cavalry battled with 
the 335th Eagles and wasted no time in crushing their formation.
  The battle ended in victory for the soldiers of Josh the gentle as they 
broke through the lines at a crucial moment.  The 352nd Light Cavalry 
scattered the 335th Eagles in all directions.
  Hindu Kush was overrun by Josh the gentle's men in a hard-fought campaign. 
The battle affected the quality of our forces:  the 435th Light Cavalry 
proved its mettle during the fight and we now rank it a Veteran unit.
  In late summer, Josh the gentle cast a spell giving magical powers of 
oratory to Josh the gentle.
  The sultry days of summer mellowed into fall.  Josh the gentle marched on 
Takla Makan (#3005) with his command.

  A host of warriors carried the banner of Josh the gentle Sevenhalf-eight 
into the Desert province of Takla Makan, held by the provincial Dragon 
chieftain.  Josh the gentle commanded the attacking armies.  The defenders 
were commanded by the provincial Dragon chieftain.  The armies were drawn up 
into lines of battle as shown here:

  ATTACKING ARMY, Battle of Takla Makan (3005):

  435th   
  Lt Cav  
